WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:04.220
In today's society it is more and more

00:00:04.220 --> 00:00:07.620
often required that we are awake at night.

00:00:09.460 --> 00:00:12.080
It may be because of shift work.

00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:14.120
It may be because we are studying very hard

00:00:14.120 --> 00:00:15.680
for an exam.

00:00:15.980 --> 00:00:17.279
To really understand what is

00:00:17.279 --> 00:00:19.350
happening when you're pulling such an

00:00:19.350 --> 00:00:21.990
all-nighter is important because society:

00:00:21.990 --> 00:00:24.210
your boss wants you to be awake at night/

00:00:24.210 --> 00:00:26.910
your biological clock want you to sleep

00:00:26.910 --> 00:00:28.190
at that particular time.

00:00:28.840 --> 00:00:31.760
Almost all organisms on Earth use internal

00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:34.460
biological clocks called circadian clocks

00:00:34.920 --> 00:00:37.160
to help them adapt to the cycle of night and day.

00:00:38.360 --> 00:00:40.220
As humans we have a circadian clock

00:00:40.220 --> 00:00:41.720
in our brain that among

00:00:41.730 --> 00:00:44.320
other things helps us to stay alert and awake

00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:46.580
during the day and to sleep at night.

00:00:47.140 --> 00:00:48.600
So what happens when we break this cycle

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:51.020
and don't go to sleep at all?

00:00:52.110 --> 00:00:53.879
Here at the University of Surrey,

00:00:53.880 --> 00:00:55.680
in the Surrey Sleep Research Centre,

00:00:55.680 --> 00:00:58.680
we have specialized facilities which allow us to

00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:00.870
study the effects of pulling an all-nighter

00:01:00.870 --> 00:01:03.630
under very controlled conditions.

00:01:04.180 --> 00:01:05.660
The participants are kept

00:01:05.670 --> 00:01:08.970
awake for 40 or 42 hours while you are

00:01:08.970 --> 00:01:12.420
lying in a bed in a dimly lit room.

00:01:12.420 --> 00:01:15.240
These bedrooms are sound attenuated, obviously

00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:17.640
light-controlled, temperature-controlled.

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:20.040
We can study the cognition in these individuals.

00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:23.640
We can simply ask them how sleepy they are.

00:01:23.640 --> 00:01:24.860
We can draw blood samples

00:01:24.860 --> 00:01:26.820
and then look at the rhythms in

00:01:26.820 --> 00:01:29.400
hormones and of course we can record

00:01:29.400 --> 00:01:33.340
their brainwaves to quantify sleep and wakefulness.

00:01:33.340 --> 00:01:36.980
We can actually see what the biological clock

00:01:36.980 --> 00:01:39.280
is telling to the rest of the body

00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:41.060
and to the rest of the brain.

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:44.340
If you keep someone awake for 40 hours straight

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:45.740
and measure their alertness,

00:01:45.740 --> 00:01:47.120
you find something surprising.

00:01:47.580 --> 00:01:50.120
Instead of alertness dropping and getting worse

00:01:50.120 --> 00:01:52.259
the longer someone is kept awake—

00:01:52.259 --> 00:01:54.030
as it gets to morning on the second day of sleep

00:01:54.030 --> 00:01:57.430
deprivation, alertness actually starts to increase again.

00:01:58.060 --> 00:01:59.180
This is because there are

00:01:59.180 --> 00:02:01.220
two forces controlling when we should sleep.

00:02:01.760 --> 00:02:04.610
The first is sleep pressure, which builds up

00:02:04.610 --> 00:02:06.690
the longer we are awake .

00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:09.380
But the second is our circadian clock, which

00:02:09.380 --> 00:02:11.390
drives alertness during the day

00:02:11.390 --> 00:02:13.630
but promote sleep at night.

00:02:13.860 --> 00:02:16.460
It's our circadian clock that gives us a fresh burst

00:02:16.460 --> 00:02:18.890
of alertness on the second day as it stops

00:02:18.890 --> 00:02:21.110
promoting sleep and starts promoting

00:02:21.110 --> 00:02:22.070
wakefulness again.

00:02:22.720 --> 00:02:24.760
So when you are a shift worker but you

00:02:24.770 --> 00:02:26.960
don't go to sleep at ten o'clock in the

00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:29.060
morning after your night shift but you

00:02:29.060 --> 00:02:31.700
stay awake as the participants in our

00:02:31.700 --> 00:02:34.190
sleep deprivation protocols do—

00:02:34.190 --> 00:02:36.470
you will find it slightly easier the second day.

00:02:36.470 --> 00:02:39.350
So despite you having been awake now

00:02:39.350 --> 00:02:44.060
for 27 or 35 hours, you can stay awake easier

00:02:44.060 --> 00:02:46.670
because the clock during the biological day

00:02:46.670 --> 00:02:49.130
will help you to stay awake.

00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:51.700
What do we learn from these studies?

00:02:51.700 --> 00:02:53.200
Well, first of all, if we understand the

00:02:53.209 --> 00:02:56.040
interaction between those circadian rhythms

00:02:56.040 --> 00:02:57.960
and our sleep/wake cycle—

00:02:57.960 --> 00:03:00.800
then we can design for example shift

00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:03.410
work schedules that will lead to less

00:03:03.410 --> 00:03:05.390
disruption of brain function.

00:03:05.390 --> 00:03:07.760
Understanding this interaction may also

00:03:07.760 --> 00:03:11.360
help us to understand why in certain

00:03:11.360 --> 00:03:14.150
neurodegenerative diseases disruption of

00:03:14.150 --> 00:03:17.030
the sleep/wake cycle is so important

00:03:17.030 --> 00:03:19.910
and how maybe by rescuing the sleep/wake cycle,

00:03:19.910 --> 00:03:22.310
we can ameliorate some of the

00:03:22.310 --> 00:03:25.260
effects of those neurodegenerative diseases

00:03:25.260 --> 00:03:28.620
or even the effects of aging itself.

00:03:28.620 --> 00:03:33.060
 

